WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:06.200
First person shooters are awesome, they're
action packed games backed by incredible graphics.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.250
And if I wanted to quickly make an fps, there's
some really great options.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.760
You've got easy tutorials in Unreal engine,
and Unity even comes with a fully featured

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.460
fps sample that works right out of the box.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.079
But what if you wanted to build one using
something else?

00:00:23.079 --> 00:00:29.570
Weirdly, anything Javascript related isn't
high, or even present, on the list of engines

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:30.999
that you might use.

00:00:30.999 --> 00:00:32.419
I feel like I can make it work though.

00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:33.419
I started by working on getting simple camera
movement working first.

00:00:33.419 --> 00:00:37.649
First person camera systems are conceptually
pretty simple, you've just got the camera

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:40.730
wedged in the guys, or girls, chest.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:48.780
And every movement happens relative to your
orientation.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.969
It doesn't take much to get a simple scene
with simple camera movement going.

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:54.570
The devil's in the details though.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:01:01.320
You can play around quite a while with little
details, like head bobbing is just a sin wave

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.150
that you apply to camera as you move, although
one little detail is that you ideally want

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.860
to stop at the end of a step, otherwise you
stop higher or lower like we've done here.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:16.210
So a little bit of extra code to dig into
those kind of details really helps.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.990
Once that's working, you may notice a problem.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:24.030
The problem is that we can just pass through
walls, we have no collision detection whatsoever,

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.130
so we'll be integrating some sort of physics
engine.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:33.040
I've been using ammo.js in my projects, which
is actually the bullet physics, a great little

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.000
open source physics engine that's good enough
for red dead redemption, so it should be more

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.940
than good enough for us too.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:44.080
This should have been easy, but in reality
it wasn't.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:50.200
The docs suck, the examples suck, everything
sucks, including me, I kinda suck at physics.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.470
Anyway with enough trawling through forums
and stack overflow, I was able to cobble together

00:01:55.470 --> 00:02:00.040
a working btKinematicCharacterController snippet
of code.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.440
And the end result was that I can run around.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.920
I can't pass through walls anymore, but I
can slide along them at angles though.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.220
I can do all the things that you'd expect,
including jump.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.830
Look I can jump, that came along as a nice
little bonus

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.300
Lets add some guns now

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.660
I grabbed this gun off of sketchfab, it looked
pretty cool so I'm happy.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.040
There's not much to making it look first person
shootery, just gotta offset it right until

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.390
it's roughly where we want it relative to
the camera.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:34.560
After that, shooting is up next, and the trick
here is to use ammo.js's raycasting abilities

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.650
to cast a ray out from our gun barrel and
see what it hits.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:43.730
So whenever I press the mouse button, we fire
off a raycast query, and figure out if we

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.540
hit anything, and then react appropriately
depending on what you hit.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:52.040
I'm not a violent guy though, so we'll make
this a paintball gun instead of a killing

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.450
people gun.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:58.940
So to get that splat effect, we could use
textures, but i'm going to hijack three.js's

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.700
shaders to do this procedurally for more variation.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:08.569
We also use three.js's decal system to procedurally
create decals from the underlying geometry.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:12.451
Now when I fire off a shot, look at that,
we do a little splat on the wall and we get

00:03:12.451 --> 00:03:13.680
our paintball effect.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.390
The cool thing about this is that it rounds
corners nicely and you get a different pattern

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.830
each time you fire a shot.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:26.610
Easing functions are an awesome little tool
to have in your toolbox as a programmer.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:32.490
Because you can use them to procedurally add
animations to shaders or meshes like magic.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:37.870
I added an exponential impulse to blend between
2 rotation quaternions.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.800
And the end result is a little firing recoil
when you take a shot.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.890
You can of course play with it, maybe recoil
more like a machine gun instead of a pistol,

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.590
up to you.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.260
The next step is that I wanted to add some
UI.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:56.650
I took Doom Eternal as my inspiration and
figured I could pretty much copy their layout.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:02.510
The nice thing about using Javascript is that
I can do this all in html and css, if you

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:04.570
wanna call that a nice thing.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:09.690
So I threw some style elements onto the screen
with 3d transforms and box shadows, and sourced

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.390
a bunch of icons from Google's free library.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.400
So we have our health and charge bars down
here.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.359
I gave this guy a chargeup power to run super
fast.

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:23.360
Ticks down the bar by just adjusting the width
and changing the colour.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.919
On the right, here's some more ui, I kinda
drew this in gimp but the rest of it is icons

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:28.919
and text.

00:04:28.919 --> 00:04:33.520
And at the top, I'm actually not quite sure
what this does in doom, so I put some random

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:34.909
icons up here.

00:04:34.909 --> 00:04:38.190
I worked on adding enemies next.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.729
I grabbed this little robot thing from Quaternius.

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:42.900
Comes fully animated already.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.569
I just plopped him into the world, but he
doesn't do anything right now, but we can

00:04:47.569 --> 00:04:49.740
fix that by adding some crude AI.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:54.250
If we give him his own collision detection
like we have for ourselves, and then throw

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:59.710
together a quick finite state machine that
has him in idle, walk, and shooting behaviours.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.930
We can really quickly and easily give him
a bit of personality.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:09.330
See, now he sees me and walks towards me,
and even tries to impotently shoot at me.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:11.509
Too bad only I have the gun.

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:15.961
So yeah of course, next thing is, I gave him
a gun, and I also coded up this force field

00:05:15.961 --> 00:05:17.800
thing using some VVoronoi noise.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:22.509
Now he's trying to take shots at me, and he
has a cool little force field around him.

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:27.439
And if I take a step back here and fire off
a few shots at him, his shield kinda changes

00:05:27.439 --> 00:05:31.830
colour and eventually he dies and I added
this quick dissolve away effect.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.610
Pretty happy how easy that was.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.669
Lets make this look better now.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:38.129
Lets make a quick level.

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:43.949
So what I'll do is quickly place down some
cubes and planes and stuff, kinda randomly.

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:48.419
We'll also place a few models that I grabbed
off of sketchfab that looked pretty cool.

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:53.240
That looks kinda neat, we've got floating
islands and different levels now, and I can

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.250
run around and jump and play, and do whatever
I want.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.779
From the corners though, you can see that
this doesn't look as good as it could.

00:06:00.779 --> 00:06:02.469
It's really apparent the shadows suck.

00:06:02.469 --> 00:06:08.059
They don't extend out very far, and we don't
get that nice shadowing on the distance blocks.

00:06:08.059 --> 00:06:13.119
Cascading shadow maps is typically the answer
to these kind of large landscapes, and as

00:06:13.119 --> 00:06:18.210
luck would have it, there are options in three.js
that I was able to integrate.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.889
Now if we peek out from the corner of the
map here, it looks great and we have shadows

00:06:21.889 --> 00:06:22.889
going on.

00:06:22.889 --> 00:06:27.589
If you look in the distance, you've got shadows,
but you've also got shadows nearby, and both

00:06:27.589 --> 00:06:29.610
of them have enough detail.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:34.710
I can also inject some of my own shader code
into three.js's for these cubes, add some

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.740
triplanar mapping and noise.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.630
That saves me from actually modelling a level.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.949
And that gets me this kinda neat futuristic
look.

00:06:41.949 --> 00:06:42.949
I like it.

00:06:42.949 --> 00:06:47.949
I also want to get some bloom in here, but
three.js doesn't quite do what I want.

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:53.159
So I copy pasted their's and I'm rewriting
it slightly to be driven by the alpha channel.

00:06:53.159 --> 00:06:57.840
That way, I can selectively bloom those light
emitting squares and even my shots have a

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.080
kind of glow to them, but nothing else does.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.199
I also played with the sky a bit.

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:07.819
What I did was combined a cubemap of a sky
with a cubemap of stars, then I drove the

00:07:07.819 --> 00:07:12.660
mix between those using the sky's luminance,
and applied some colour grading.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:17.490
There's also a bit of rotation using time,
and I added for that small bit of movement

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.319
to keep it from being totally static.

00:07:19.319 --> 00:07:24.589
I also bloom out the sun manually via the
shader, so you get sort of a bleed over effect

00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:26.710
in that direction.

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:31.960
This also means that those force fields on
the droids, they glow a bit now. which improves

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:32.960
the look quite a bit.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:36.449
I also took the opportunity to add a little
radial blur effect.

00:07:36.449 --> 00:07:41.530
It has a simple falloff from the center with
a bit of basic colour manipulation.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:43.289
And here it is in action.

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:48.169
When activated, I contrast the center and
destaurate a bit towards the sides.

00:07:48.169 --> 00:07:51.179
I dunno, see what works for you.

00:07:51.179 --> 00:07:55.080
There's also a subtle vignette around the
edges of the screen because I like the look.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.719
Anyway, thats about as far as I feel like
taking this.

00:07:58.719 --> 00:07:59.719
It works.

00:07:59.719 --> 00:08:01.740
I've got the beginnings of something here.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:05.770
Maybe somebody wants to actually make this
into a real game.

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:10.669
So the code will be available on github, look
for an annoucement on twitter for when its

00:08:10.669 --> 00:08:11.669
available.

00:08:11.669 --> 00:08:12.729
Until next time, cheers.

